---
// StorageScale.astro — "the claim, drawn to scale".
// Replaces the old stats band + abstract flow diagram. Instead of telling
// the visitor numbers, it draws what ten uploads of the same artifact do
// to a bucket: full copies on the left, one reference + hairline deltas on
// the right. The figures are the verified 61,400%-smaller datapoint family
// used site-wide (91 MB artifact → 148 KB delta), and the v100 projection
// lands on the real 8,850%-smaller catalog number from the migration log —
// on purpose.
import ScaleChart from './ScaleChart.astro';
---

<section class="section scale">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2 class="scale-title">What ten releases do to a bucket</h2>
    <p class="scale-sub">
      These are the same ten uploads of a 91&nbsp;MB build artifact, stored
      two ways. The bars are proportional. We draw the deltas with a
      two-pixel floor, or they would be invisible.
    </p>

    <ScaleChart />

    <p class="scale-projection">
      The gap widens with every release. By v100 it's 9.1&nbsp;GB against
      about 106&nbsp;MB, which is 8,600% less. Our own release catalog landed
      at <a href="/case-studies/readonlyrest-builds">8,850% smaller on a
      real release line</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="scale-mech">
      The proxy stores each upload as a binary diff against a full
      reference copy that it keeps beside it, and every read rebuilds the
      exact original. All of this sits behind the same S3 API your tools
      already speak.
    </p>

    <aside class="scale-objection">
      <p class="objection-q">
        “Our artifacts are ZIPs and JARs. They're already compressed, so
        diffing them should do nothing.”
      </p>
      <p class="objection-a">
        It would, on random bytes. But versioned archives aren't random.
        From one version to the next, the same entries land in the same
        order (the manifest, the icons, the bundled CSS), so the binary diff
        stays tiny. The 91&nbsp;MB artifact in the chart <em>is</em> a
        compressed ZIP: a real ReadonlyREST plugin, stored 61,400% smaller.
        <a href="/docs/explanation/delta-compression">The engineering
        note</a> explains why.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .scale-title { margin-top: 0; }

  .scale-sub {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: 0 0 2.25rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .scale-projection {
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  .scale-mech {
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .scale-objection {
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brand);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .objection-q {
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    font-style: italic;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .objection-a {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: none;
  }

</style>
